BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Public Talk: The Rise of Civilizational Narratives in Contemporary
  China
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T133000
DTSTAMP:20260903T140208Z
UID:dartmouth-82429@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82429\n\nDescription:\nThe department of history is pleased to an
 nounce the following talk by Prof. Dominic Sachsenmaier (University of Gö
 ttingen): The Rise of Civilizational Narratives in Contemporary China: Glo
 bal Contexts and Historical Genealogies.\nSponsored by:\nThe Harris Progra
 m\nLunch will be provided. Please RSVP to Megan Yeo (Megan.G.Yeo@dartmouth
 .edu).\nAbstract:\nDuring the past two decades\, the concept of “civiliz
 ation” has regained prominence in China: state authorities\, the media\,
  and academic circles frequently invoke the notion of Chinese civilization
  (wenming). In a first step\, this talk examines selected variants of this
  renewed civilizational discourse and the groups that promote them. In a s
 econd step\, it reflects on the historical genealogies and more recent dri
 vers of civilizational narratives in China. Finally\, it seeks to situate 
 developments in China within broader global trends: in many parts of the w
 orld\, “civilization” has once again become a socially contested and p
 olitically mobilized term.\nSpeaker Biography:\nDominic Sachsenmaier is a 
 chair professor of “Modern China with a Special Emphasis on Global Histo
 rical Perspectives”. Before coming to Göttingen\, he held faculty posit
 ions at Jacobs University\, Duke University and UC Santa Barbara. Dominic 
 Sachsenmaier is an elected member of the European Academy of Sciences and 
 Arts and he is also one of the three editors of the book series „Columbi
 a Studies in International and Global History“ (Columbia UP). His main r
 esearch interests include China’s transnational and global connections i
 n the past and present. For instance\, he authored the monographs “Globa
 l Perspectives on Global History” (Cambridge UP\, 2011)\, and “Global 
 Entanglements of a Man Who Never Traveled” (Columbia UP\, 2018). His new
  book (“Modern China and its Worlds”) will be published by Cambridge U
 niversity Press in 2027.\n\nSponsor / organizer: History Department\n\nAud
 ience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: explicit food
 -service wording\; food keyword near service context\n\nFood availability 
 is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260903T140208Z
LOCATION:Carson Hall 312
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=82429
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
END:VEVENT
