DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82205\n\nDescription:\nEdmund Burke is often seen as the founding
  father of modern conservatism. How is his “conservatism” similar to o
 r different from what we call conservatism in our own time? Prof. Gregory 
 M. Collins—Lecturer and Director of Undergraduate Studies in the Program
  on Ethics\, Politics\, and Economics at Yale—is well placed to address 
 this question. An award-winning teacher and scholar\, his research ranges 
 widely over the origins of liberalism and conservatism. His book\nCommerce
  and Manners in Edmund Burke’s Political Economy\nappeared with Cambridg
 e in 2020\, and he is now writing a book on the idea of civil society in e
 arly African-American political thought. Co-sponsored by the Rockefeller C
 enter for Public Policy.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Political Economy Project
 \n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: Free F
 ood category\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source listing\; ve
 rify details before attending.
